   "source": [
    "# **To what extent do various atmospheric emissions contribute to climate change indicators, including regional and global temperature variations and global sea level change?**\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "zHT4IulDBmfd"
   },
   "source": [
    "## Hypothesis/analysis goal (Ask)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "tu-SRMFWBvxw"
   },
   "source": [
    "As global industrialisation continues to advance, atmospheric emissions have increased significantly over time. At the same time, changes in climate patterns, including rising temperatures and sea level variations, have become more evident. While it is widely recognized that human activities influence the climate system, the relationships between different types of atmospheric emissions and climate indicators remain complex and interdependent. These emissions may exhibit varying associations with regional and global temperature patterns as well as long-term sea level changes. Therefore, this study aims to explore and analyze the relationships and trends between multiple atmospheric emissions and key climate change indicators across different regions and at a global scale, with a focus on understanding their temporal dynamics.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "VX_60E5tLaIc"
   },
   "source": [
    "## Data source identification and exploration (Prepare)\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "B1DrggRmLhXa"
   },
   "source": [
    "**Requirement analysis**: The greenhouse gases we are using for this analysis are Carbon Dioxide and Methane, as they are the main greenhouse gases. For each of them, we need data on emission by each country, emission per year and yearly changes in emission per country.\n",
    "\n",
    "For weather patterns, our data should include different weather indicators for each country. Different weather indicators include precipitation, atmospheric pressure, wind speed, wind direction  and temperature. By merging these atmospheric and meteorological datasets, we can perform a multi-dimensional correlation analysis to identify how specific gases drive changes in regional climate stability and determine which factors have the most significant impact on global weather patterns\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "g8BH4X-AL0uo"
   },
   "source": [
    "**List of considered sources:**\n",
    "- [Earth Surface Temperature Data by Berkeley Earth](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/berkeleyearth/climate-change-earth-surface-temperature-data?select=GlobalLandTemperaturesByCity.csv)\n",
    "- [Daily Weather Data](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/guillemservera/global-daily-climate-data/data?select=daily_weather.parquet)\n",
    "- [Methane Emissions Data](https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/methane-emissions?tab=table&time=earliest..2024&country=OWID_ASI~OWID_NAM~OWID_SAM~OWID_EUR~USA~RUS)\n",
    "- [Deforestation Data](https://ourworldindata.org/deforestation)\n",
    "- [Carbon Dioxide Emissions Data](https://ourworldindata.org/co2-dataset-sources)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "TJNNjMlWO7YY"
   },
   "source": [
