   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "rVshAtd6JABl"
   },
   "source": [
    "There are 216 countries worth of data over the timespan from 1950s to 2023."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "3MJK_eixJABl"
   },
   "source": [
    "Null Values Analysis"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "id": "wDJWZTtkJABl"
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "null_counts = weather.isnull().sum()\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "plt.bar(null_counts.index, (null_counts.values / len(weather)) * 100)\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Columns\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Number of Null Values (% of total)\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Histogram of Null Values per Column\")\n",
    "plt.xticks(rotation=45, ha='right')  # Rotate x-axis labels for readability\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "JX31ED0tJABl"
   },
   "source": [
    "Observe that some of these columns have near 95% null values, hence they might not be too useful in our analysis. It might even affect our analysis by giving us false positives / false negatives which we want to avoid. However, keep in mind that the data spans from as early as 1950s and therefore it is expected for some data to be missing from that time period (we can choose the timeframe we want to work with). Some of these columns are not that significant in our analysis either, so we can drop them when need be. However, if we can find ways to work with the data (e.g replacement of NULL values), then we will consider using them in our analysis. The columns that we can consider using for our analysis at this point is temperature and precipitation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "le5YpMz5JABl"
   },
   "source": [
    "COUNTRY DATA ANALYSIS"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "id": "NFM_5vCCJABl"
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "weather.groupby('country')['station_id'].count().plot()\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Countries\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Maximum number of data\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Graph of maximum number of data per country\")\n",
    "plt.axhline(y=0, color='red', linestyle='--', linewidth=1, label='y = 0')\n",
    "plt.xticks(rotation=90, ha='right', fontsize=10)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "z7XIoFV0JABl"
   },
   "source": [
    "In the graph above, we use 'station_id' as the y-axis to show the maximum number of data a country can provide, since station_id has no null values in our dataset. We observe that some countries have close to 0 number of data to offer, so we can get rid of them in our analysis as it provides little to no value for us. We also observe that some countries have exponentially more data to offer than the rest, which might cause some overrepresentation in our analysis. We can consider choosing countries with the average maximum number of data, with some room for error, so as to maximise the number of countries in our analysis as well as to avoid over / under representation of data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "id": "LuRolMjlJABl"
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "country_counts = weather.groupby('country')['country'].count().sort_values(ascending=True)\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(12, 6))\n",
    "plt.bar(country_counts.head(50).index, country_counts.head(50).values)\n",
    "plt.xlabel('Country')\n",
    "plt.ylabel('Data given per country')\n",
    "plt.title('Bottom 50 Countries with Least Data given')\n",
    "plt.xticks(rotation=90)  # Rotate x-axis labels for readability\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "eZP5LTb6JABl"
   },
   "source": [
    "From the graph above, we can see the bottom 50 countries with the least data offered, with the lowest being 2500, out of 2.8million. We can also see there is a variety of countries in different regions, such as Singapore and Brunei in Asia, Somalia in Africa, and Andorra in Europe, to name a few. Hence this dataset fits our requirement in terms of variablility and broadness."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "E8hfIFP-JABl"
   },
   "source": [
    "TEMPERATURE ANALYSIS"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "id": "nWUeN0YyJABl"
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Keep only needed columns\n",
    "cols_to_agg = ['date', 'avg_temp_c', 'min_temp_c', 'max_temp_c']\n",
    "weather_small = weather[cols_to_agg]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Resample yearly\n",
    "df_yearly = weather_small.resample('YE', on='date').agg({\n",
    "    'avg_temp_c': 'mean',\n",
    "    'min_temp_c': 'min',\n",
    "    'max_temp_c': 'max'\n",
    "}).reset_index()\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(12,6))\n",
    "plt.plot(df_yearly['date'].dt.year, df_yearly['avg_temp_c'], label='Average Temp')\n",
    "plt.plot(df_yearly['date'].dt.year, df_yearly['min_temp_c'], label='Min Temp')\n",
    "plt.plot(df_yearly['date'].dt.year, df_yearly['max_temp_c'], label='Max Temp')\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.title('Yearly Temperature Statistics')\n",
    "plt.xlabel('Year')\n",
    "plt.ylabel('Temperature (°C)')\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.grid(True)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "MI6XlDxOJABm"
   },
   "source": [
    "From the graph above, we can see that there is not just the average temperature, but also the minimum and maximum, which gives us a range of temperatures to work with rather than one static value. This will give us more information about temperature fluctuations per year. However, from our null analysis, we note that there are some null values within temperatures, which we will keep in mind when doing our analysis."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "8GmpV7rgJABm"
   },
   "source": [
    "#### **Quality Considerations**"
