   "source": [
    "### Basic Understanding"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Emissions Overview**\n",
    "\n",
    "Each of the emissions in our dataset has different sources and impacts on climate change. Here’s a brief overview of each gas:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **BC (Black Carbon)**: A component of fine particulate matter from incomplete combustion (e.g., diesel, biomass). It strongly absorbs sunlight, warming the atmosphere and darkening snow/ice, accelerating melting.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **CH₄ (Methane)**: A powerful greenhouse gas with high short-term warming potential. Emitted from agriculture (livestock), fossil fuels, and landfills. Much stronger than CO₂ over ~20 years but shorter-lived.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **CO (Carbon Monoxide)**: An indirect greenhouse gas. It doesn’t trap heat strongly itself but affects atmospheric chemistry, increasing methane lifetime and contributing to ozone formation.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **CO₂ (Carbon Dioxide)**: The primary greenhouse gas driving long-term climate change. Released mainly from fossil fuel burning, deforestation, and industrial processes. Long atmospheric lifetime.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **N₂O (Nitrous Oxide)**: A potent greenhouse gas and ozone-depleting substance. Emitted mainly from agricultural soils, fertilizers, and some industrial activities. Long-lived and very strong warming effect.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **NH₃ (Ammonia)**: Not a greenhouse gas directly, but contributes to air pollution by forming fine particles (PM2.5). Mostly from agriculture (livestock waste and fertilizers).\n",
    "\n",
    "- **NMVOC (Non-Methane Volatile Organic Compounds)**: A group of organic gases (excluding methane) that contribute to the formation of ground-level ozone and secondary organic aerosols, indirectly affecting climate.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **NOₓ (Nitrogen Oxides: NO and NO₂)**: Play a key role in forming ozone and secondary particles. Indirectly influence climate by affecting methane and ozone levels.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **OC (Organic Carbon)**: A component of particulate matter from combustion. Unlike black carbon, it tends to reflect sunlight, having a cooling effect, though it also affects cloud formation.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **SO₂ (Sulfur Dioxide)**: Forms sulfate aerosols that reflect sunlight, leading to a cooling effect. However, it contributes to acid rain and has harmful health impacts."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Descriptive"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Global-level"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Given that our overarching goal is to determine the extent to which greenhouse gas emissions and other human factors correlate with multi-dimensional weather patterns globally, we apply the following methods:\n",
    "\n",
    "*   Temperature trend analysis \n",
    "*   Temperature range and extremes analysis \n",
    "*   Emissions overview and trajectory analysis \n",
    "*   Sea level trend analysis "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
