    "print(f\"Columns        : {list(df.columns)}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Missing values : {df.isnull().sum().sum()}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Display summary statistics for all variables\n",
    "df.describe().T.round(3)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Historical Era Segmentation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Since our dataset covers a wide range of years, from 1880s to 2015, we want to divide this range into different eras, since there has been historical notable events that can potentially affect trends of our data (gas emissions, etc). We do this so that we can gain more valuable insights about our data and relate it back to these historical events, rather than just treating it as a datapoint in our analysis. The following are the eras we considered, and will be in our analysis from now on."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "ERAS = [\n",
    "    (1880, 1914, '#d4e6f1', 'Industrial Acceleration'),\n",
    "    (1914, 1945, '#fdebd0', 'War & Disruption'),\n",
    "    (1945, 1973, '#d5f5e3', 'Post-War Boom'),\n",
    "    (1973, 1990, '#fdf2b0', 'Oil Shocks & Env. Awareness'),\n",
    "    (1990, 2008, '#f9ebea', 'Globalisation & Rapid Growth'),\n",
    "    (2008, 2010, '#e8daef', 'Financial Crisis'),\n",
    "    (2010, 2015, '#eafaf1', 'Climate Policy Era'),\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "ERA_EVENTS = {\n",
    "    1914: 'WWI',\n",
    "    1945: 'WWII End',\n",
    "    1973: 'Oil Crisis',\n",
    "    1990: 'Globalisation',\n",
    "    2008: 'Financial Crisis',\n",
    "    2015: 'Paris Agreement',\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "def add_era_bands(ax, alpha=0.18):\n",
    "    #shows our different eras in different colors\n",
    "    for start, end, color, label in ERAS:\n",
    "        ax.axvspan(start, end, alpha=alpha, color=color, zorder=0)\n",
    "\n",
    "def add_era_lines(ax, ypos_frac=0.97, fontsize=7):\n",
    "    #to show clear division between our eras in our visualisations\n",
    "    ylim = ax.get_ylim()\n",
    "    yrange = ylim[1] - ylim[0]\n",
    "\n",
    "    for year, label in ERA_EVENTS.items():\n",
    "        if ax.get_xlim()[0] <= year <= ax.get_xlim()[1]:\n",
    "            ax.axvline(year, color='grey', lw=0.8, ls='--', alpha=0.6, zorder=1)\n",
    "            ax.text(\n",
    "                year + 0.5,\n",
    "                ylim[0] + ypos_frac * yrange,\n",
    "                label,\n",
    "                fontsize=fontsize,\n",
    "                color='grey',\n",
    "                va='top'\n",
    "            )\n",
    "\n",
    "#column groupings used throughout\n",
    "EMISS_COLS = ['CO2', 'CH4', 'N2O', 'SO2', 'NOx', 'CO', 'BC', 'OC', 'NH3', 'NMVOC']\n",
    "\n",
    "EMISS_COLORS = [\n",
    "    '#e74c3c','#8e44ad','#2980b9','#f39c12','#27ae60',\n",
    "    '#e67e22','#1abc9c','#c0392b','#7f8c8d','#3498db'\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "EMISS_UNITS = {\n",
    "    'CO2': 'Mt CO₂',\n",
    "    'CH4': 'Mt CH₄',\n",
    "    'N2O': 'Mt N₂O',\n",
    "    'SO2': 'kt SO₂',\n",
    "    'NOx': 'kt NOₓ',\n",
    "    'CO':  'kt CO',\n",
    "    'BC':  'kt BC',\n",
    "    'OC':  'kt OC',\n",
    "    'NH3': 'kt NH₃',\n",
    "    'NMVOC': 'kt NMVOC'\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "CLIMATE_COLS = [\n",
    "    'LandAverageTemperature',\n",
    "    'LandAndOceanAverageTemperature',\n",
    "    'LandMaxTemperature',\n",
    "    'LandMinTemperature',\n",
    "    'Mean Sea Level'\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "#historical era label lookup (used in scatter plots)\n",
    "era_labels = [\n",
    "    'Industrial Acc.',\n",
    "    'War & Disruption',\n",
    "    'Post-War Boom',\n",
    "    'Oil Shocks',\n",
    "    'Globalisation',\n",
    "    'Financial Crisis',\n",
    "    'Policy Era'\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "era_years = [\n",
    "    (1880, 1914),\n",
    "    (1914, 1945),\n",
    "    (1945, 1973),\n",
    "    (1973, 1990),\n",
    "    (1990, 2008),\n",
    "    (2008, 2010),\n",
    "    (2010, 2015)\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "def get_era_label(yr):\n",
    "    for (s, e), lab in zip(era_years, era_labels):\n",
    "        if s <= yr < e:\n",
    "            return lab\n",
    "    return 'Policy Era'\n",
    "\n",
    "ds_global['era'] = ds_global['year'].apply(get_era_label)\n",
    "df['era'] = df['year'].apply(get_era_label)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Era segmentation complete. Eras:\", era_labels)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Seven historical eras spanning 1880-2015 are defined. Every major chart in this notebook uses these bands as a reference layer, so that visual trends can be immediately connected to their underlying historical drivers."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Temperature Trend Analysis"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Since we want to analyse the effects on gas emissions on temperature, we must first observe the temperature trend over the years in our dataset. This will be the basis of all our analysis. We also want to observe the rate of acceleration and the uncertainty.\n",
    "\n",
    "We use a multi-panel time-series line chart with ±1σ uncertainty bands and a 10-year rolling mean. The rolling mean smooths year-to-year natural variability (ENSO, volcanic eruptions) and reveals the underlying long-term signal. Two panels separate land-only and land+ocean measurements, since oceans act as a thermal buffer and produce a physically distinct baseline. Each panel plots the raw annual series, its uncertainty range, and its 10-year rolling mean, overlaid with historical era bands and event markers."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(14, 9), sharex=True)\n",
    "fig.suptitle('Global Temperature Trends (1880-2015)', fontsize=15, fontweight='bold', y=1.01)\n",
    "\n",
    "panels = [\n",
    "    ('LandAverageTemperature', 'LandAverageTemperatureUncertainty',\n",
    "     'Land Average Temperature', '#e74c3c', '#fadbd8'),\n",
    "    ('LandAndOceanAverageTemperature', 'LandAndOceanAverageTemperatureUncertainty',\n",
    "     'Land + Ocean Average Temperature', '#2980b9', '#d6eaf8'),\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "for ax, (col, unc_col, title, color, fill_color) in zip(axes, panels):\n",
    "    add_era_bands(ax)\n",
    "    ax.fill_between(df['year'],\n",
    "                    df[col] - df[unc_col],\n",
    "                    df[col] + df[unc_col],\n",
    "                    alpha=0.3, color=fill_color, label='±1σ Uncertainty')\n",
    "    ax.plot(df['year'], df[col], color=color, lw=1.8, label=title)\n",
    "    ax.plot(df['year'], df[col].rolling(10, center=True).mean(),\n",
    "            'k--', lw=2.2, label='10-yr Rolling Mean')\n",
    "    add_era_lines(ax)\n",
    "    ax.set_ylabel('Temperature (°C)', fontsize=10)\n",
    "    ax.set_title(title, fontsize=12)\n",
    "    ax.legend(fontsize=9, loc='upper left')\n",
    "\n",
    "axes[1].set_xlabel('Year', fontsize=10)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "There is a clear upward trend from 1910 onwards, with an acceleration between World War II. Also, we note that the steepest rise in the rolling mean in the Globalisation era, which is the fastest sustained warming of the entire record. It also seems like land temperatures tend to rise faster than the combined land and ocean temperatures, possibly because the ocean's large heat capacity slows down warming.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations:** Since we are doing a global scale analysis, this means that we are taking averages from different regions. In other words, some regions might warm up faster, or slower than the global mean, and are not reflected in our global analysis.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Conclusion:** Global temperatures have increased by approximately **1.7 °C for land** and **0.9 °C for land and ocean** over our years, with a notable acceleration in the second half of the 20th century, which we will seek to explain in our later analysis."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Temperature Range and Extremes Analysis"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now, we want to determine if the increase in both minimum and maximum temperatures are equal (symmetric) or one rises faster than the other (asymmetric). This analysis will allow us to determine the effects that gas emissions might have on temperature, since greenhouse gases tend to reduce cooling at night, making nighttime temperature (minimum) warmer than daytime temperature (maximum).\n",
    "\n",
    "We overlay land maximum, average, and minimum temperatures on a single filled area chart to visualise the spread and its evolution. We also compute the temperature range (max − min) per era to quantify whether the distribution is widening or narrowing.\n",
    "\n",
    "A filled area chart is used because it communicates the width of the temperature spread intuitively through area rather than just lines."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "df['TempRange'] = df['LandMaxTemperature'] - df['LandMinTemperature']\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(14, 6))\n",
    "add_era_bands(ax)\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.fill_between(df['year'], df['LandMinTemperature'], df['LandMaxTemperature'],\n",
    "                alpha=0.25, color='#e74c3c', label='Max-Min Range')\n",
    "ax.plot(df['year'], df['LandMaxTemperature'], color='#c0392b', lw=1.4, label='Land Max Temp')\n",
    "ax.plot(df['year'], df['LandAverageTemperature'], color='#e74c3c', lw=2, label='Land Avg Temp')\n",
    "ax.plot(df['year'], df['LandMinTemperature'], color='#f1948a', lw=1.4, label='Land Min Temp')\n",
    "\n",
    "add_era_lines(ax)\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Year', fontsize=10)\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Temperature (°C)', fontsize=10)\n",
    "ax.set_title('Land Temperature Extremes and Range (1880-2015)', fontsize=13, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "ax.legend(fontsize=9)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#we get a summary of the mean temp values per era, and put it in a table for easy referencing\n",
    "rows = []\n",
    "for (s, e), lab in zip(era_years, era_labels):\n",
    "    sub = df[(df['year'] >= s) & (df['year'] < e)]\n",
    "    rows.append({\n",
    "        'Era': lab,\n",
    "        'Avg Temp (°C)': round(sub['LandAverageTemperature'].mean(), 3),\n",
    "        'Max Temp (°C)': round(sub['LandMaxTemperature'].mean(), 3),\n",
    "        'Min Temp (°C)': round(sub['LandMinTemperature'].mean(), 3),\n",
    "        'Range (°C)':    round(sub['TempRange'].mean(), 3),\n",
    "    })\n",
    "pd.DataFrame(rows)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We observe that both maximum and minimum temperatures have increased over the years, so the increase is not limited to daytime peaks only. Also, the temperature range has remained quite stable across the years, which means that both minimum and maximum have been rising at similar rates globally. We can see that the increase is more evident/obvious after the end of WWII, and most notably from Globalisation era. In recent decades, it also seems like the rate at which the minimum temperature rises is increasing more than that of the maximum temperature, which can be explained by greenhouse gases effect on reduced cooling at night.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations:** Similar to our first analysis, some regions can warm up faster or slower than the global average temperature, for instance Artic regions which tends to warm up 2-3 times faster. Regions like this might be underrepresented in our global averages.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Conclusion:** Warming is occurring across the full temperature spectrum (max,min,mean) and not just during peak conditions. Temperature rise is also near-symmetric in both extremes (min and max), which again can be explained by greenhouse effect rather than a daytime heat effect."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Emissions Overview and Trajectory Analysis"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now that we have analysed temperature, we want to move on to analysing the gas emissions trends and how they have changed over our period of time. This is so that we can understand the scale of each gases and the rate at which they change, and whether there is a decline or not. Only then can we relate these gas emissions to our temperature changes that we have observed.\n",
    "\n",
    "A multi panel time-series chart is used, with one panel per emission variable. Separate panels are required because different variables here have different units and scales, so plotting all these together will make smaller variables invisible, affecting our analysis. A 10-year rolling mean is also applied to each panel so as to reveal the underlying trend beneath the annual noise."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(5, 2, figsize=(16, 22), sharex=True)\n",
    "axes = axes.flatten()\n",
    "fig.suptitle('Global Emissions by Pollutant (1880-2015)', fontsize=16, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "\n",
    "for ax, col, color in zip(axes, EMISS_COLS, EMISS_COLORS):\n",
    "    add_era_bands(ax, alpha=0.15)\n",
    "    ax.plot(df['year'], df[col], color=color, lw=1.8)\n",
    "    ax.plot(df['year'], df[col].rolling(10, center=True).mean(),\n",
    "            'k--', lw=1.5, alpha=0.7)\n",
    "    add_era_lines(ax, ypos_frac=0.96, fontsize=6)\n",
    "    ax.set_title(col, fontsize=11, fontweight='bold', color=color)\n",
    "    ax.set_ylabel(EMISS_UNITS.get(col, ''), fontsize=8)\n",
    "\n",
    "for ax in axes[-2:]:\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel('Year', fontsize=9)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "From these 10 emission variables, we observe that we can divide them into 3 different behavioural groups.\n",
    "\n",
    "The first group (CO₂, CH₄, N₂O), seems to show a sustained and almost exponential increase from the mid-20th century. These 3 greenhouse gases have been accumulating in the atmosphere over centuries, and hence their trajectories seems to match closely to the temperature record.\n",
    "\n",
    "The second group (SO₂, NOₓ, CO) shows a rise and fall pattern, where emissions seem to rise and peak around 1970s to 1980s, and then fall afterwards. There are multitude of potential reasons for this, mainly the oil crisis, clean air legislations and the shift to cleaner energy.\n",
    "\n",
    "The third group (NH₃, BC, OC, NMVOC) reflects growing economies, through agriculture and combustion. NH₃ accelerates after 1990, possibly due to increased synthetic fertiliser use. BC also rises, due to industrial combustion and diesel engines."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Sea Level Trend Analysis"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We want to track sea level as well, because it acts as a multi-dimensional indicator of climate change. Sea level shows the consequences of climate change in different ways, such as thermal expansion of ocean water and melting of ice due to warming. We are using sea level also because it is less prone to year-to-year noise than land temperature.\n",
    "\n",
    "We use a two-panel layout combines a long-term trend line with a year-on-year bar chart. The bar chart immediately reveals whether the rate of rise is accelerating or decelerating over time.\n",
    "\n",
    "The upper panel shows the raw series and its 10-year rolling mean with era bands. The lower panel shows annual incremental change as a bar chart, with positive changes (rising) coloured red and negative changes coloured blue."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(14, 8), sharex=True,\n",
    "                                gridspec_kw={'height_ratios': [3, 1]})\n",
    "fig.suptitle('Global Mean Sea Level (1880-2015)', fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "\n",
    "add_era_bands(ax1)\n",
    "ax1.plot(df['year'], df['Mean Sea Level'], color='#2980b9', lw=2, label='Mean Sea Level (mm)')\n",
    "ax1.plot(df['year'], df['Mean Sea Level'].rolling(10, center=True).mean(),\n",
    "         'k--', lw=2, label='10-yr Rolling Mean')\n",
    "ax1.axhline(0, color='grey', lw=0.8, ls=':')\n",
    "add_era_lines(ax1)\n",
    "ax1.set_ylabel('Sea Level Anomaly (mm)', fontsize=10)\n",
    "ax1.legend(fontsize=9)\n",
    "\n",
    "diffs = df['Mean Sea Level'].diff()\n",
    "colors_bar = ['#e74c3c' if v > 0 else '#2980b9' for v in diffs]\n",
    "ax2.bar(df['year'], diffs, color=colors_bar, alpha=0.7)\n",
    "ax2.axhline(0, color='k', lw=0.8)\n",
    "ax2.set_ylabel('Annual Change (mm)', fontsize=9)\n",
    "ax2.set_xlabel('Year', fontsize=10)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "total_rise = df['Mean Sea Level'].iloc[-1] - df['Mean Sea Level'].iloc[0]\n",
    "print(f\"Total sea level change (1880-2015): {total_rise:.1f} mm  ({total_rise/10:.1f} cm)\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Sea level has increased by approximately 230mm (23cm) over the course of the dataset. The trend is almost entirely upwards, with a few dips that can potentially be explained by natural phenomenon like volcanic cooling that temporarily slows down ocean warming. Looking at the bar chart, it shows mostly positive annual changes from the 1990s onwards, clearly indicating a clear acceleration in the rate of sea level rise during the Globalisation era.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations:** Does not capture regional variations of sea level since we are using global averages. Local sea level can deviate significantly from global mean due to factors like ice sheet mass loss and ocean circulation.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Conclusion:** Sea level rise has been increasing over the years, with an increased rate of increase in recent decades, which can be linked to the increase in greenhouse gases as observed in the previous analysis."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Normalised Trajectory Comparison"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We now want to compare the increase of all the emission variables against temperature and sea level by plotting, so we can visualise and identify the emissions that most closely track the climate response curve over time. We will use normalisation because these emissions are recorded in different scales and units, making direct comparison not very feasible.\n",
    "\n",
    "Min-max normalisation rescales every variable to the range [0, 1], where 0 represents the historical minimum and 1 represents the historical maximum. This removes unit differences entirely and allows a direct visual comparison of growth shapes and rates. Temperature and sea level are plotted as thick lines; emissions as thinner coloured lines."
   ]
