    "During the mid-to-late 19th century (particularly 1860-1880), the Tropical cohort experienced extreme, high-amplitude temperature swings (e.g., sharp drops and rapid recoveries). This era of intense volatility was driven by two primary natural forcings:\n",
    "\n",
    "- ENSO Extremes: Severe El Niño-Southern Oscillation (ENSO) phases heavily impacted these regions. Intense La Niña episodes drove sharp cooling along the Pacific coast (Ecuador/Peru) and altered South Asian monsoons (Pakistan), while corresponding El Niño phases caused rapid upward temperature spikes.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Volcanic Cooling: Clusters of mid-century volcanic eruptions injected stratospheric aerosols, culminating in the massive 1883 Krakatoa eruption. Krakatoa caused a visible multi-year stabilization dip due to widespread global cooling, which temporarily suppressed temperature averages until the late 1880s.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Persistent High-Latitude Volatility\n",
