   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Correlation Matrix Analysis"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now that we have analysed each variable separately, we want to find out how all the variables connect to each other. Firstly, we will find the correlation to see how strong are the relationships between each variable.\n",
    "\n",
    "Pearson correlation coefficients are computed across all 15 variables (10 emissions + 5 climate indicators) and visualised as an annotated heatmap. Pearson r is appropriate here because all variables are continuous, and the relationships appear broadly linear from the time-series inspection above."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "df = ds_global"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "analysis_cols = EMISS_COLS + CLIMATE_COLS\n",
    "corr = df[analysis_cols].corr()\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(16, 13))\n",
    "im = ax.imshow(corr, cmap=plt.cm.RdBu_r, vmin=-1, vmax=1, aspect='auto')\n",
    "\n",
    "for i in range(len(corr)):\n",
    "    for j in range(len(corr)):\n",
    "        val = corr.iloc[i, j]\n",
    "        color = 'white' if abs(val) > 0.65 else 'black'\n",
    "        ax.text(\n",
    "            j, i, f'{val:.2f}',\n",
    "            ha='center', va='center',\n",
    "            fontsize=7.5,\n",
    "            color=color,\n",
    "            fontweight='bold' if abs(val) > 0.8 else 'normal'\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "labels = [\n",
    "    c.replace('LandAndOcean', 'L+O\\n')\n",
    "     .replace('LandAverage', 'LandAvg\\n')\n",
    "     .replace('LandMax', 'LandMax\\n')\n",
    "     .replace('LandMin', 'LandMin\\n')\n",
    "     .replace('AverageTemperature', 'Temp')\n",
    "     .replace('Temperature', 'Temp')\n",
    "     .replace('Mean Sea Level', 'Sea\\nLevel')\n",
    "    for c in analysis_cols\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_xticks(range(len(analysis_cols)))\n",
    "ax.set_xticklabels(labels, fontsize=8, rotation=45, ha='right')\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_yticks(range(len(analysis_cols)))\n",
    "ax.set_yticklabels(labels, fontsize=8)\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_title(\n",
    "    'Correlation Matrix: Emissions & Climate Variables (1880-2015)',\n",
    "    fontsize=14,\n",
    "    fontweight='bold',\n",
    "    pad=15\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "fig.colorbar(im, ax=ax, fraction=0.03, pad=0.02).set_label('Pearson r', fontsize=10)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#display the correlation of each variable with Land Avg Temp, in decreasing order\n",
    "print(\"Correlations with Land Average Temperature (ranked):\")\n",
    "temp_corr = corr['LandAverageTemperature'].drop('LandAverageTemperature').sort_values(ascending=False)\n",
    "print(temp_corr.round(4).to_string())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The matrix reveals that CO₂, CH₄, N₂O, and mean sea level shows the highest positive correlation with all the temperature variables, with r > 0.83. This confirms their dominant role as long-lived greenhouse gases in the atmosphere that traps outgoing heat. Mean sea level has strong correlation with these same gases, which shows consistency with thermal expansion and melting of ice due to temperature increase.\n",
    "\n",
    "SO₂ and NOₓ have weaker or even negative partial correlations with temperature, implying that these gases can partially offset warming. This is not surprising as SO₂ form reflective aerosol particles that can deflect heat that enters the atmosphere. Hence, their post-1980 decline seems to have unmasked additional greenhouse warming rather than contributing to it.\n",
    "\n",
    "BC shows a strong positive correlation because it directly absorbs solar radiation rather than acting as a greenhouse gas, but still ultimately increasing the overall temperature.\n",
    "\n",
    "Additionally, OC (organic carbon) has been ranked one of the highest in terms of its correlation. However, it should not be considered a direct warming agent. In fact, it is co-emitted with BC from the same combustion sources, so its high correlation actually reflects the shared combustion activity rather than an independent climate mechanism. OC's behaviour is also quite ambiguous, in the sense that it contributes to cooling via solar radiation, and also alters cloud formation.\n",
    "\n",
    "All in all, all the emission variables here are highly correlated with one another as well, which reflects their common driver which is economic growth and thus increased economic activity and global energy use.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations:** Correlation does not imply causation. These variables might not necessarily have a linear relationship with one another. The high correlation between emission variables might be overstated as well, since all are driven by economic growth instead of the direct causal relationships between the gases themselves.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Conclusion:** Long-lived greenhouse gases and mean sea level have very strong correlation to temperature change, and SO2 has a potential negative moderator of climate change, which we will look at further into our diagnostics analysis.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Bivariate Scatter Plot Analysis"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    " We now know roughly how strong the relationship is like between our variables. Now we want to find out what the relationship actually looks like, so we can observe the linearity, clusters (by eras) and also potential outliers.\n",
    "\n",
    "Scatter plots are produced for the five most analytically significant emission variables (CO₂, CH₄, N₂O, SO₂, BC) against land average temperature. Each point is colour-coded by historical era, adding a third dimension that shows whether the relationship is consistent across time or era-dependent. An ordinary least squares (OLS) trend line is overlaid on each panel."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "ERA_COLORS_POINT = {\n",
    "    'Industrial Acc.': '#3498db',\n",
    "    'War & Disruption': '#e67e22',\n",
    "    'Post-War Boom':    '#27ae60',\n",
    "    'Oil Shocks':       '#f39c12',\n",
    "    'Globalisation':    '#e74c3c',\n",
    "    'Financial Crisis': '#8e44ad',\n",
    "    'Policy Era':       '#1abc9c',\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "TARGET_EMISS  = ['CO2', 'CH4', 'N2O', 'SO2', 'BC']\n",
    "TARGET_LABELS = ['CO₂ (Mt)', 'CH₄ (Mt)', 'N₂O (Mt)', 'SO₂ (kt)', 'BC (kt)']\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 5, figsize=(22, 5))\n",
    "fig.suptitle('Bivariate: Key Emissions vs. Land Average Temperature — by Historical Era',\n",
    "             fontsize=13, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "\n",
    "for ax, col, lbl in zip(axes, TARGET_EMISS, TARGET_LABELS):\n",
    "    for era, grp in df.groupby('era'):\n",
    "        ax.scatter(grp[col], grp['LandAverageTemperature'],\n",
    "                   c=ERA_COLORS_POINT.get(era, 'grey'),\n",
    "                   alpha=0.85, s=28, label=era, zorder=2)\n",
    "    # OLS trend line\n",
    "    x, y = df[col], df['LandAverageTemperature']\n",
    "    xline = np.linspace(x.min(), x.max(), 200)\n",
    "    ax.plot(xline, np.polyval(np.polyfit(x, y, 1), xline),\n",
    "            'k--', lw=1.5, alpha=0.7)\n",
    "    r = np.corrcoef(x, y)[0, 1]\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel(lbl, fontsize=9)\n",
    "    ax.set_ylabel('Land Avg Temp (°C)' if col == 'CO2' else '', fontsize=9)\n",
    "    ax.set_title(f'{col}\\nr = {r:.3f}', fontsize=10, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "\n",
    "handles = [mpatches.Patch(color=c, label=l) for l, c in ERA_COLORS_POINT.items()]\n",
    "fig.legend(handles=handles, loc='lower center', ncol=7, fontsize=8,\n",
    "           bbox_to_anchor=(0.5, -0.08), title='Historical Era')\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "CO₂, CH₄, and N₂O plots shows tight and nearly positive relationship with temperature. The blue dots (Industrial Acceleration era) cluster at the lower left, while red dots (Globalisation era) cluster at the upper right, which shows clear temporal progression and confirms that both emissions and temperature rise together over time.\n",
    "\n",
    "SO₂ shows weaker relationship, and is points are more scattered. This can be attributed to the rise up till 1970s when temperature was also rising (positive relationship), but then declining post 1970s due to regulations while temperature continued rising (negative relationship), so this disrupts the positive relationship that they initially had.\n",
    "\n",
    "BC also shows a positive relationship, but points are also more scattered than the greenhouse gases. This can be attributed to the different sources of black carbon combustion such as wildfires and diesel engines, whose geographical distribution and seasonal timing varies.\n",
    "\n",
    "In all the plots, all dots from the same era are clustered together, indicating that era-level structural economic changes drive both emissions and temperature at the same time.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations:** Both emissions and temperature are variables affected by time, so some of the apparent correlation here may reflect their shared upward trend rather than a direct mechanism. There's also lag effects that are not captured (emissions take a period of time to affect temperature)."
   ]
  },
