    "##### Decadal Rate-of-Change Analysis"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We now want to find out how fast temperature, CO₂, and sea level have been changing per decade. This is so that we can determine if these rates are accelerating, as well as to see whether the warming has kept up, lagged behind or exceeded the rate of CO₂ growth.\n",
    "\n",
    "Annual data is aggregated to decadal means, and the change between consecutive decades is computed as a measure of decadal acceleration. Bar charts are used because they effectively communicate discrete categorical comparisons (decade-by-decade). Negative changes are highlighted in grey to make reversals immediately visible."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "df['decade'] = (df['year'] // 10) * 10\n",
    "decade_df = df.groupby('decade')[['LandAverageTemperature', 'CO2', 'Mean Sea Level']].mean().reset_index()\n",
    "decade_df['Temp_Change']   = decade_df['LandAverageTemperature'].diff()\n",
    "decade_df['CO2_Change']    = decade_df['CO2'].diff()\n",
    "decade_df['SeaLvl_Change'] = decade_df['Mean Sea Level'].diff()\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(3, 1, figsize=(13, 12), sharex=True)\n",
    "fig.suptitle('Decadal Rates of Change: Temperature, CO₂, and Sea Level',\n",
    "             fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "\n",
    "metrics = [\n",
    "    ('Temp_Change',   'Decadal Temperature Change (°C)', '#e74c3c'),\n",
    "    ('CO2_Change',    'Decadal CO₂ Change (Mt)',          '#8e44ad'),\n",
    "    ('SeaLvl_Change', 'Decadal Sea Level Change (mm)',    '#2980b9'),\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "for ax, (col, ylabel, color) in zip(axes, metrics):\n",
    "    bars = ax.bar(decade_df['decade'], decade_df[col], color=color, alpha=0.8, width=8)\n",
    "    for bar, val in zip(bars, decade_df[col]):\n",
    "        if not np.isnan(val) and val < 0:\n",
    "            bar.set_color('lightgrey')\n",
    "            bar.set_edgecolor(color)\n",
    "    ax.axhline(0, color='k', lw=0.8)\n",
    "    ax.set_ylabel(ylabel, fontsize=9)\n",
    "\n",
    "axes[-1].set_xlabel('Decade', fontsize=10)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#summary table\n",
    "decade_df[['decade', 'LandAverageTemperature', 'CO2',\n",
    "           'Temp_Change', 'CO2_Change', 'SeaLvl_Change']].round(3)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "From the decadal data, global temperature shows a clear long-term warming trend, with positive changes in nearly every decade since 1910. The largest increases occur in recent decades, particularly from the 1980s onward. CO₂ levels exhibit strong acceleration, with the 2000s showing the largest decadal increase in the dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "A notable exception occurs around the 1940s-1950s, where temperature temporarily decreases despite rising CO₂ levels. This can be explained by factors such as increased aerosol emissions (e.g., SO₂) during wartime industrial activity, which can temporarily offset warming.\n",
    "\n",
    "Sea level rise also demonstrates an accelerating pattern, with the most recent decades showing the largest increases.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Conclusion:** All three variables (temperature, CO₂, sea level) show consistent upward trends with signs of acceleration. While this dataset alone cannot establish causation, the observed patterns are consistent with established scientific understanding that increasing greenhouse gas concentrations contribute to global warming and rising sea levels."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Composite Human Pressure vs. Climate Response Index"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now that we pretty much have an idea about how the emissions affect temperature, their relationships, how they have changed throughout decades and eras, we want to summarise everything together. We will do so by constructing a single integrated measure of the collective weight of all the emissions (Human Pressure Index -> HPI), and compare it to a single integrated measure of multi-dimensional climate response (Climate Response Index - CRI). Essentially, if these 2 indices are closely enough to one another, this will show that the combined human emissions actually drives the overall climate response.\n",
    "\n",
    "Both indices are constructed by min-max normalising each component variable to [0, 1] and then taking the arithmetic mean across components. The HPI averages all ten emission variables; the CRI averages land average temperature and mean sea level. The Pearson correlation between HPI and CRI is then computed as the primary summary statistic."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#build Human Pressure Index (HPI): average of all normalised emission variables\n",
    "df_n2 = df[EMISS_COLS].copy()\n",
    "for c in df_n2.columns:\n",
    "    mn, mx = df_n2[c].min(), df_n2[c].max()\n",
    "    df_n2[c] = (df_n2[c] - mn) / (mx - mn)\n",
    "df['HPI'] = df_n2.mean(axis=1)\n",
    "\n",
    "#build Climate Response Index (CRI): average of normalised temperature and sea level\n",
    "clim_cols = ['LandAverageTemperature', 'Mean Sea Level']\n",
    "df_c2 = df[clim_cols].copy()\n",
    "for c in df_c2.columns:\n",
    "    mn, mx = df_c2[c].min(), df_c2[c].max()\n",
    "    df_c2[c] = (df_c2[c] - mn) / (mx - mn)\n",
    "df['CRI'] = df_c2.mean(axis=1)\n",
    "\n",
    "#r value between HPI and CRI\n",
    "r_hpi_cri = np.corrcoef(df['HPI'], df['CRI'])[0, 1]\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(14, 6))\n",
    "add_era_bands(ax)\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.plot(df['year'], df['HPI'], color='#e74c3c', lw=2.2, label='Human Pressure Index (HPI)')\n",
    "ax.plot(df['year'], df['CRI'], color='#2980b9', lw=2.2, label='Climate Response Index (CRI)')\n",
    "ax.fill_between(df['year'], df['HPI'], df['CRI'],\n",
    "                where=df['HPI'] >= df['CRI'], alpha=0.2, color='#e74c3c',\n",
    "                label='HPI > CRI (pressure ahead of response)')\n",
    "ax.fill_between(df['year'], df['HPI'], df['CRI'],\n",
    "                where=df['HPI'] <  df['CRI'], alpha=0.2, color='#2980b9',\n",
    "                label='CRI > HPI (lagged response)')\n",
    "\n",
    "add_era_lines(ax)\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Year', fontsize=10)\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Normalised Index (0-1)', fontsize=10)\n",
    "ax.set_title('Human Pressure Index vs. Climate Response Index (1880-2015)',\n",
    "             fontsize=13, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "ax.legend(fontsize=9, loc='upper left')\n",
    "ax.text(0.98, 0.05, f'r = {r_hpi_cri:.4f}', transform=ax.transAxes,\n",
    "        ha='right', fontsize=11, fontweight='bold',\n",
    "        bbox=dict(boxstyle='round,pad=0.3', facecolor='wheat', alpha=0.6))\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "print(f\"Pearson r (HPI vs CRI): {r_hpi_cri:.4f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can observe, from the graph as well as the r-value, that both the HPI and CRI have a very strong relationship with each other (r value of > 0.93) and therefore they track each other very well. This indicates that the combined human emissions throughout time is mirrored by the climate response, at a global scale. To put it simply, no individual gas nor climate variable like temperature, can produce this level of integrated agreement. This emerges from the combined weight of all the anthropogenic pressures that have been acting simultaneously on the climate system.\n",
    "\n",
    "We observe that in the earlier decades (1880 to 1940), CRI exceeds HPI by quite a margin, meaning that emissions are still quite low as compared to the current temperature at that time. Essentially, the climate system started off from a warmer baseline, while the gas emissions started at near 0 (quite low).\n",
    "\n",
    "From 1960s onwards, it seems that HPI over CRI, possibly due to the massive increase in gas emissions at a faster rate than the climate system can actually respond. The gap here has gotten smaller, which can potentially be explained by continued heat uptake by the ocean and lag effect, as well as suppression of warming by aerosol (SO₂).\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations:** We are combining variables with different mechanisms, behaviours and timescales into one composite index. This might cause certain issues, for instance CO₂ which is a multi century forcer and SO₂ which is a forcer that clears from the atmosphere in weeks, causing the index to treat them as equivalent despite them having completely different behaviours and timescales. However, we are doing this to summarise and conclude our findings after doing all analysis.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Conclusion:** We conclude that human emissions (gases) has indeed affected the climate over time, as described by the indices in the graph. The correlation is very strong at r > 0.97. This also clearly shows that the increase in temperature and sea level is NOT coincidence nor the work of Nature alone."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Ranking for emissions\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "ds_global.columns"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
    "from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "gas_cols = ['BC', 'CH4', 'CO2', 'CO', 'N2O', 'NH3', 'NMVOC', 'NOx', 'OC', 'SO2']\n",
    "\n",
    "# --- Step 1: Pearson Correlation ---\n",
    "correlations = {}\n",
    "for gas in gas_cols:\n",
    "    # Assuming ds_global is defined earlier in your script\n",
    "    corr = ds_global[gas].corr(ds_global['LandAverageTemperature'])\n",
    "    correlations[gas] = abs(corr)  # absolute value to capture both warming and cooling\n",
    "\n",
    "corr_df = pd.DataFrame.from_dict(correlations, orient='index', columns=['Abs_Correlation'])\n",
    "corr_df['Corr_Rank'] = corr_df['Abs_Correlation'].rank(ascending=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "# --- Data Preparation ---\n",
    "# Note: Kept X, y, and scaling here as they are required for the Random Forest step\n",
    "X = ds_global[gas_cols].fillna(0)\n",
    "y = ds_global['LandAverageTemperature']\n",
    "\n",
    "scaler = StandardScaler()\n",
    "X_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X)\n",
    "\n",
    "# --- Step 2: Random Forest Feature Importance ---\n",
    "rf = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=100, random_state=42)\n",
    "rf.fit(X_scaled, y)\n",
    "\n",
    "rf_df = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    'Gas': gas_cols,\n",
    "    'RF_Importance': rf.feature_importances_\n",
    "}).set_index('Gas')\n",
    "rf_df['RF_Rank'] = rf_df['RF_Importance'].rank(ascending=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "# --- Step 3: Composite Priority Score ---\n",
    "# Joined only the remaining Corr_Rank and RF_Rank\n",
    "final_priority = corr_df[['Corr_Rank']].join(rf_df['RF_Rank'])\n",
    "final_priority['Composite_Score'] = final_priority.mean(axis=1)\n",
    "final_priority = final_priority.sort_values('Composite_Score', ascending=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "# --- Visualisation ---\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "bars = plt.barh(final_priority.index, final_priority['Composite_Score'], color='steelblue')\n",
    "plt.gca().invert_yaxis()\n",
    "plt.xlabel('Composite Priority Score (higher = more important to regulate)')\n",
    "plt.title('Gas Prioritisation: Composite Score Across Correlation & Random Forest')\n",
    "plt.grid(axis='x', alpha=0.3)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Print statement updated to reflect removed columns\n",
    "print(final_priority[['Corr_Rank', 'RF_Rank', 'Composite_Score']])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Sea level\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor\n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "\n",
    "# Define features — gases only, explicitly excluding all temperature columns\n",
    "gas_cols = ['BC', 'CH4', 'CO2', 'CO', 'N2O', 'NH3', 'NMVOC', 'NOx', 'OC', 'SO2']\n",
    "\n",
    "# Drop rows where sea level or any gas is null\n",
    "df_sea = ds_global[gas_cols + ['Mean Sea Level']].dropna()\n",
    "\n",
    "X = df_sea[gas_cols]\n",
    "y = df_sea['Mean Sea Level']\n",
    "\n",
    "# Standardise features\n",
    "scaler = StandardScaler()\n",
    "X_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Train Random Forest\n",
    "rf_sea = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=100, random_state=42)\n",
    "rf_sea.fit(X_scaled, y)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Extract and rank feature importances\n",
    "importance_df = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    'Variable': gas_cols,\n",
    "    'Importance': rf_sea.feature_importances_\n",
    "}).sort_values('Importance', ascending=False).reset_index(drop=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(importance_df)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Visualise\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "plt.barh(importance_df['Variable'], importance_df['Importance'], color='steelblue')\n",
    "plt.gca().invert_yaxis()\n",
    "plt.xlabel('Feature Importance Score')\n",
    "plt.title('Gases Affecting Mean Sea Level (Random Forest, Descending Order)')\n",
    "plt.grid(axis='x', alpha=0.3)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Continent-level"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Continental-Level Emission-Temperature Relationships"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "df = ds_country.copy()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#Linear regression model between all the greenhouse gases emission and the average temperature\n",
    "\n",
    "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import r2_score    \n",
    "\n",
    "X = df[gas_cols]\n",
    "y = df['AvgTemp']\n",
    "model = LinearRegression()\n",
    "model.fit(X, y)\n",
    "y_pred = model.predict(X)\n",
    "r2 = r2_score(y, y_pred)\n",
    "print(f\"R-squared: {r2:.4f}\")\n",
    "coefficients = pd.Series(model.coef_, index=gas_cols)\n",
    "print(\"Coefficients:\")\n",
    "print(coefficients.sort_values(ascending=False))\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Loop through each continent to answer your specific research question\n",
    "results = []\n",
    "\n",
    "for continent, group in ds_country.groupby('continent'):\n",
    "    X = group[gas_cols]\n",
    "    y = group['AvgTemp']\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Check if there's enough data to run a regression\n",
    "    if len(group) < 10:\n",
    "        continue\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Scale the features so coefficients are comparable!\n",
    "    scaler = StandardScaler()\n",
    "    X_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Fit the model\n",
    "    model = LinearRegression()\n",
    "    model.fit(X_scaled, y)\n",
    "    y_pred = model.predict(X_scaled)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Record the R-squared for this specific region\n",
    "    r2 = r2_score(y, y_pred)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Store results in a dictionary\n",
    "    continent_data = {'continent': continent, 'R-squared': r2}\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Add the scaled coefficients\n",
    "    for i, col in enumerate(gas_cols):\n",
    "        continent_data[col + '_coef'] = model.coef_[i]\n",
    "\n",
    "    results.append(continent_data)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Convert results to a DataFrame for easy viewing\n",
    "regional_impact_df = pd.DataFrame(results)\n",
    "print(regional_impact_df)"
   ]
