  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Hypothesis Testing\n",
    "\n",
    "To rigorously examine the relationship between greenhouse gas emissions and temperature at the country level, we conduct statistical hypothesis tests. These tests help us determine whether observed differences in temperature across emission levels are statistically significant or due to random variation.\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### T-Test - Comparing Temperatures Between High and Low CO2 Emitters"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We divide countries into two groups based on their historical CO2 emissions (above/below median) and test whether their average temperatures differ significantly."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Calculate total historical CO2 emissions per country\n",
    "country_total_co2 = ds_country.groupby('country')['CO2'].sum()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Create binary classification: high vs low emitters\n",
    "median_emissions = country_total_co2.median()\n",
    "high_emitters = country_total_co2[country_total_co2 > median_emissions].index\n",
    "low_emitters = country_total_co2[country_total_co2 <= median_emissions].index\n",
    "\n",
    "# Get average temperatures for each group\n",
    "high_emitter_temps = ds_country[ds_country['country'].isin(high_emitters)]['AvgTemp']\n",
    "low_emitter_temps = ds_country[ds_country['country'].isin(low_emitters)]['AvgTemp']\n",
    "\n",
    "# Perform independent t-test\n",
    "t_stat, p_value = stats.ttest_ind(high_emitter_temps, low_emitter_temps, equal_var=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"=\" * 60)\n",
    "print(\"T-TEST: Temperature Comparison Between High and Low CO2 Emitters\")\n",
    "print(\"=\" * 60)\n",
    "print(f\"\\nHigh Emitters (n={len(high_emitters)} countries):\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Mean Temperature: {high_emitter_temps.mean():.2f}°C\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Std Deviation: {high_emitter_temps.std():.2f}°C\")\n",
    "print(f\"\\nLow Emitters (n={len(low_emitters)} countries):\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Mean Temperature: {low_emitter_temps.mean():.2f}°C\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Std Deviation: {low_emitter_temps.std():.2f}°C\")\n",
    "print(f\"\\nT-Statistic: {t_stat:.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"P-Value: {p_value:.4e}\")\n",
    "print(f\"\\nResult: {'Statistically Significant' if p_value < 0.05 else 'Not Statistically Significant'} at α = 0.05\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Interpretation of Hypothesis Testing Results:\n",
    "\n",
    "The T-test results reveal whether countries with higher CO2 emissions experience significantly different temperatures compared to low emitters. A statistically significant result (p < 0.05) would suggest that emission levels are associated with temperature differences at the country level, supporting our research hypothesis.\n",
    "\n",
    "These hypothesis tests provide the statistical foundation for our investigation. If significant differences are found, they justify the need for more sophisticated diagnostic models (like the regression analyses that follow) to understand the nature and strength of the emission-temperature relationship across different global regions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Emissions Priority Analysis"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "EMISSIONS = ['BC', 'CH4', 'CO', 'CO2', 'N2O', 'NH3', 'NMVOC', 'NOx', 'OC', 'SO2']\n",
    "GLOBAL_TEMP_COL = 'LandAndOceanAverageTemperature'\n",
    "COUNTRY_TEMP_COL = 'AvgTemp'"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Stationarity Check (ADF Test)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Since Granger casuality and VAR requires the data to be stationary, we will conduct ADF test to check for stationarity of our data. The null hypothesis of the ADF test is that the data is non-stationary, so if the p-value is less than 0.05, we can reject the null hypothesis and conclude that the data is stationary. If data is non-stationary, we will need to apply differencing to make it stationary before we can proceed."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def check_stationarity(df, cols):\n",
    "    results = []\n",
    "    for col in cols:\n",
    "        series = df[col].dropna()\n",
    "        adf_stat, p, *_ = adfuller(series, autolag='AIC')\n",
    "        results.append({\n",
    "            'column': col,\n",
    "            'ADF Statistic': round(adf_stat, 4),\n",
    "            'p-value': round(p, 4),\n",
    "            'Stationary (p<0.05)': p < 0.05\n",
    "        })\n",
    "    return pd.DataFrame(results).set_index('column')\n",
    "\n",
    "def make_stationary(df, cols, stat_df):\n",
    "    df2 = df.copy()\n",
    "    differenced = []\n",
    "    for col in cols:\n",
    "        if not stat_df.loc[col, 'Stationary (p<0.05)']:\n",
    "            df2[col] = df2[col].diff()\n",
    "            differenced.append(col)\n",
    "    return df2.dropna().reset_index(drop=True), differenced\n",
    "\n",
    "all_cols = [GLOBAL_TEMP_COL] + EMISSIONS\n",
    "stat_df = check_stationarity(ds_global, all_cols)\n",
    "\n",
    "display(stat_df)\n",
    "\n",
    "df_stat, differenced = make_stationary(ds_global, all_cols, stat_df)\n",
    "if differenced:\n",
    "    print(f'\\nFirst-differenced (non-stationary): {differenced}')\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    print('\\nAll series are stationary — no differencing needed')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# plt df_stat to visually confirm stationarity\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(14, 12))\n",
    "gs = GridSpec(len(all_cols), 1)\n",
    "for i, col in enumerate(all_cols):\n",
    "    ax = plt.subplot(gs[i])\n",
    "    sns.lineplot(data=df_stat, x='year', y=col, ax=ax)\n",
    "    ax.set_title(f'{col}')\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel('')\n",
    "    ax.set_ylabel('')\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We see that all columns is now stationary. This allows us to proceed with our Granger causality and VAR analysis."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Granger Causality Test"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Granger causality test is a statistical hypothesis test for determining whether one time series can predict another. In our case, we want to test whether the emissions of different gases can predict changes in temperature. The null hypothesis is that the emissions do not Granger-cause temperature, and if the p-value is less than 0.05, we can reject the null hypothesis and conclude that there is a Granger causality relationship between the emissions and temperature.\n",
    "\n",
    "Question: Does past values of this emission help predict temperature better than temperature alone?\n",
    "\n",
    "The function test multiple lag lengths (40 in this case) using the SSR F-tests, extracts p-values and F-statistics for each lag, identifies the optimal lag based on the lowest p-value, and summarizes the results."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def run_granger(df, temp_col, emissions, max_lag=5):\n",
    "    results = []\n",
    "    for em in emissions:\n",
    "        sub = df[[temp_col, em]].dropna()\n",
    "        gc = grangercausalitytests(sub, maxlag=max_lag, verbose=False)\n",
    "        pvals = [gc[lag][0]['ssr_ftest'][1] for lag in range(1, max_lag + 1)]\n",
    "        fvals = [gc[lag][0]['ssr_ftest'][0] for lag in range(1, max_lag + 1)]\n",
    "        best_lag = np.argmin(pvals) + 1\n",
    "        results.append({\n",
    "            'emission': em,\n",
    "            'min_pvalue': min(pvals),\n",
    "            'best_lag': best_lag,\n",
    "            'max_F_stat': max(fvals),\n",
    "            'granger_causes_temp': min(pvals) < 0.05\n",
    "        })\n",
    "    return pd.DataFrame(results).sort_values('min_pvalue')\n",
    "\n",
    "granger_df = run_granger(df_stat, GLOBAL_TEMP_COL, EMISSIONS, max_lag=40)\n",
    "granger_df['C'] = -np.log10(granger_df['min_pvalue'].clip(lower=1e-10))\n",
    "\n",
    "granger_df"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The emissions are then ranked based on their minimum p-value across all lags, allowing us to identify which emissions have the strongest predictive relationship with temperature changes. We then plot results using -log10(p-value) to visually represent the strength of the Granger causality."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 5), facecolor='#0d1117')\n",
    "gdf = granger_df.sort_values('min_pvalue')\n",
    "colors = ['palegreen' if v else 'lightcoral' for v in gdf['granger_causes_temp']]\n",
    "\n",
    "bars = ax.barh(gdf['emission'], -np.log10(gdf['min_pvalue'].clip(1e-10)), color=colors)\n",
    "ax.axvline(-np.log10(0.05), color='yellow', linestyle='--', lw=1.5, label='p = 0.05 threshold')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Annotate best lag\n",
    "for bar, (_, row) in zip(bars, gdf.iterrows()):\n",
    "    ax.text(bar.get_width() + 0.05, bar.get_y() + bar.get_height()/2,\n",
    "            f'lag={int(row[\"best_lag\"])}y', va='center', color='white', fontsize=8)\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_title('Granger Causality - Does Emission X Predict Future Temperature?',\n",
    "             color='white', fontsize=11)\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('-log₁₀(p-value)  ->  higher = stronger causal signal', color='white')\n",
    "ax.set_facecolor('#161b22')\n",
    "ax.tick_params(colors='white')\n",
    "ax.legend(fontsize=9, labelcolor='white', facecolor='#0d1117')\n",
    "for spine in ax.spines.values(): spine.set_color('#30363d')\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We not only get influence of each emission on temperature, but also the optimal lag at which this influence is strongest, which can provide insights into the temporal dynamics of how emissions affect temperature.\n",
    "\n",
    "The Granger causality results show that only N2O (p = 0.0429) and NMVOC (p = 0.0489) significantly Granger-cause temperature, indicating that their past values provide useful predictive information for temperature. Both effects appear over long lag structures, suggesting delayed influence. This implies that changes in Nitrous oxide (which is a potent greenhouse gas) and Non-Methane Volatile Organic Compounds (NMVOC) emissions may have a more prolonged impact on temperature, potentially due to their atmospheric lifetimes or indirect effects on climate processes. The other emissions do not show significant Granger causality, suggesting that their influence on temperature may be more immediate or confounded by other factors."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Forecast Error Variance Decomposition (FEVD)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Forecast Error Variance Decomposition (FEVD) is a technique used in the context of Vector Autoregression (VAR) models to understand the contribution of each variable to the forecast error variance of another variable over a specified horizon. In our case, we want to see how much of the forecast error variance of temperature can be attributed to shocks in each emission variable.\n",
    "\n",
    "Question: How much of the forecast error variance of temperature can be explained by shocks to each emission variable over a 10-year horizon?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def run_fevd(df, temp_col, emissions, horizon=40):\n",
    "    cols = [temp_col] + emissions\n",
    "    data = df[cols].dropna()\n",
    "    model = VAR(data)\n",
    "    lag_order = model.select_order(maxlags=10)\n",
    "    best_p = min(lag_order.aic, 6)\n",
    "    best_p = max(best_p, 1)  # at least 1 lag\n",
    "    print(f'VAR optimal lag order (AIC): {best_p}')\n",
    "    fitted = model.fit(best_p)\n",
    "    fevd = fitted.fevd(horizon)\n",
    "    # decomp[-1, 0, :] = final horizon, temperature equation, all shocks\n",
    "    temp_fevd = fevd.decomp[-1, 0, :]\n",
    "\n",
    "    y = data[temp_col].iloc[best_p:].values\n",
    "    y_hat = fitted.fittedvalues[temp_col].values\n",
    "    ss_res = np.sum((y - y_hat) ** 2)\n",
    "    ss_tot = np.sum((y - np.mean(y)) ** 2)\n",
    "    r2 = 1 - ss_res / ss_tot\n",
    "    print(f'VAR R² for {temp_col}: {r2:.4f}')\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return pd.Series(temp_fevd, index=cols).drop(temp_col).sort_values(ascending=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "fevd_series = run_fevd(df_stat, GLOBAL_TEMP_COL, EMISSIONS, horizon=40)\n",
    "\n",
    "fevd_pct = (fevd_series / fevd_series.sum() * 100).round(2)\n",
    "display(fevd_pct.to_frame('% Variance Explained'))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(14, 5), facecolor='black')\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_facecolor('#161b22')\n",
    "cmap = plt.cm.viridis \n",
    "wedge_colors = [cmap(i) for i in np.linspace(0, 1, len(fevd_pct))]\n",
    "wedges, texts, autotexts = ax.pie(\n",
    "    fevd_pct.sort_values(ascending=False),\n",
    "    labels=fevd_pct.sort_values(ascending=False).index,\n",
    "    autopct='%1.1f%%',\n",
    "    colors=wedge_colors,\n",
    "    textprops={'color': 'white', 'fontsize': 9}\n",
    ")\n",
    "for at in autotexts: at.set_color('white')\n",
    "ax.set_title('FEVD Share', color='white', fontsize=11)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "FEVD results show highly uneven distribution of influence across emissions. BC is the dominant contributor, suggesting that it carries the strongest independent dynamic signal in explaining fluctuations of the target variable within the VAR framework. SO2 is the second most important factor, followed by CO2, CH4, and CO, indicating moderate but substantially smaller roles in driving system variability. In contrast, all remaining pollutants contribute less than 1.5% individually. Overall, the decomposition reveals a strongly skewed structure in which a single variable (BC) dominates the system’s short-term dynamics.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Conclusion:** Black carbon (BC) has the strongest contribution to short-run unpredictable movements (shocks) in the target variable. This suggests that BC emissions have a more immediate and significant impact on temperature fluctuations compared to other pollutants. The high contribution of BC may be due to its strong absorption of solar radiation, which can lead to rapid warming effects in the atmosphere."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "However, FEVD was excluded because it captures variance attribution, which overlaps with Granger's predictive causality test and lacks physical units, making it less interpretable in the context of our analysis. It tell how emissions expplain the variance of temperature, but it does not tell us about the magnitude of the effect nor the direction of the relationship, which is what we are more interested in for our analysis."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Impulse Response Function (IRF)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Impulse Response Function (IRF) is a tool used in the context of Vector Autoregression (VAR) models to analyze how a shock to one variable affects the future values of itself and other variables in the system over time. In our case, we want to see how a shock to each emission variable affects temperature over a 10-year horizon. \n",
    "\n",
    "Question: How much does temperature respond move after emission shock?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def run_irf(df, temp_col, emissions, periods=20):\n",
    "    results = []\n",
    "\n",
    "    for em in emissions:\n",
    "        sub = df[[temp_col, em]].dropna()\n",
    "\n",
    "        model  = VAR(sub)\n",
    "        fitted = model.fit(maxlags=5, ic='aic')\n",
    "        irf    = fitted.irf(periods=periods)\n",
    "        \n",
    "        em_idx   = list(sub.columns).index(em)\n",
    "        temp_idx = list(sub.columns).index(temp_col)\n",
    "        \n",
    "        response = irf.irfs[:, temp_idx, em_idx]\n",
    "        \n",
    "        # get confidence intervals via bootstrap\n",
    "        irf_err  = irf.errband_mc(orth=False, repl=100, signif=0.05)\n",
    "        lower    = irf_err[0][:, temp_idx, em_idx]\n",
    "        upper    = irf_err[1][:, temp_idx, em_idx]\n",
    "        \n",
    "        peak_response = np.max(np.abs(response))\n",
    "        cum_response  = np.sum(np.abs(response))\n",
    "        \n",
    "        results.append({\n",
    "            'emission':       em,\n",
    "            'peak_response':  peak_response,\n",
    "            'cum_response':   cum_response,\n",
    "            'peak_at_period': np.argmax(np.abs(response)),\n",
    "            'response':       response.tolist(),\n",
    "            'lower':          lower.tolist(),\n",
    "            'upper':          upper.tolist()\n",
    "        })\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return pd.DataFrame(results).sort_values('cum_response', ascending=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "irf_df = run_irf(df_stat, GLOBAL_TEMP_COL, EMISSIONS, periods=20)\n",
    "irf_df"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 5, figsize=(20, 8))\n",
    "axes = axes.flatten()\n",
    "\n",
    "for i, row in irf_df.iterrows():\n",
    "    response = np.array(row['response'])\n",
    "    lower    = np.array(row['lower'])\n",
    "    upper    = np.array(row['upper'])\n",
    "    x        = range(len(response))\n",
    "    \n",
    "    axes[i].plot(x, response, color='darkorange', linewidth=2)\n",
    "    axes[i].fill_between(x, lower, upper, alpha=0.2, color='darkorange')\n",
    "    axes[i].axhline(0, color='black', linewidth=0.8, linestyle='--')\n",
    "    axes[i].set_title(row['emission'])\n",
    "    axes[i].set_xlabel('Periods after shock')\n",
    "    axes[i].set_ylabel('Temp response (°C)')\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.suptitle('IRF: Temperature Response to Emission Shock', fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Cross-Correleation Function (CCF)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def run_ccf(df, temp_col, emissions, max_lag=40):\n",
    "    results = []\n",
    "    \n",
    "    for em in emissions:\n",
    "        sub = df[[temp_col, em]].dropna()\n",
    "        temp = sub[temp_col].values\n",
    "        emis = sub[em].values\n",
    "        \n",
    "        ccf_vals = []\n",
    "        for lag in range(0, max_lag + 1):\n",
    "            if lag == 0:\n",
    "                r, _ = stats.pearsonr(emis, temp)\n",
    "            else:\n",
    "                r, _ = stats.pearsonr(emis[:-lag], temp[lag:])\n",
    "            ccf_vals.append(r)\n",
    "        \n",
    "        ccf_vals    = np.array(ccf_vals)\n",
    "        peak_corr   = np.max(np.abs(ccf_vals))\n",
    "        best_ccf_lag = np.argmax(np.abs(ccf_vals))\n",
    "        mean_corr   = np.mean(np.abs(ccf_vals))\n",
    "        std_corr    = np.std(np.abs(ccf_vals))\n",
    "        L           = (peak_corr - mean_corr) / std_corr if std_corr > 0 else 0\n",
    "        \n",
    "        results.append({\n",
    "            'emission':     em,\n",
    "            'peak_corr':    peak_corr,\n",
    "            'best_ccf_lag': best_ccf_lag,\n",
    "            'L_score':      L,\n",
    "            'ccf_vals':     ccf_vals  # store full CCF for potential plotting\n",
    "        })\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return pd.DataFrame(results).sort_values('L_score', ascending=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "ccf_df = run_ccf(df_stat, GLOBAL_TEMP_COL, EMISSIONS, max_lag=40)\n",
    "ccf_df"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def plot_ccf(ccf_df, max_lag=40):\n",
    "    fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 5, figsize=(20, 8))\n",
    "    axes = axes.flatten()\n",
    "    \n",
    "    for i, row in ccf_df.iterrows():\n",
    "        ccf_vals = np.array(row['ccf_vals'])\n",
    "        best_lag = row['best_ccf_lag']\n",
    "        \n",
    "        axes[i].bar(range(max_lag + 1), ccf_vals, color='steelblue', alpha=0.7)\n",
    "        axes[i].axvline(best_lag, color='red', linestyle='--', label=f'Peak lag={best_lag}')\n",
    "        axes[i].axhline(0, color='black', linewidth=0.8)\n",
    "        axes[i].set_title(row['emission'])\n",
    "        axes[i].set_xlabel('Lag (years)')\n",
    "        axes[i].set_ylabel('Correlation')\n",
    "        axes[i].legend(fontsize=8)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    plt.suptitle('CCF: Emissions → Temperature (Lag Structure)', fontsize=14, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "    plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "    plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "plot_ccf(ccf_df)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### CIS Score Calculation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The **Climate Impact Score (CIS)** is a composite index designed to quantify the causal relationship between greenhouse gas emissions and temperature change using three orthogonal components. Each component answers a distinct scientific question, ensuring no redundancy in what the score captures.\n",
    "\n",
    "The first component, **Causality Strength (C)**, is derived from Granger causality testing and measures the statistical evidence that emissions help predict future temperature. A higher C score indicates stronger predictive causality. The second component, **Temporal Dependency Strength (L)**, uses cross-correlation analysis to measure how clearly a single lag dominates the emission-temperature relationship. A high L score reflects a stable, consistent response delay in the climate system. The third component, **Shock Response Magnitude (E)**, is computed from Impulse Response Function analysis and captures how much temperature physically moves following an emission shock, representing the real-world scale of impact.\n",
    "\n",
    "The final score is computed as a weighted sum of the three normalized components: **CIS = w₁C + w₂L + w₃E**, with equal weights applied by default. By combining statistical causality, temporal structure, and physical impact magnitude into a single interpretable number, the CIS allows direct comparison of climate impact across different emission types, free from black-box methods and with each component remaining independently meaningful."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def minmax(s):\n",
    "    return (s - s.min()) / (s.max() - s.min()) if s.max() != s.min() else s * 0\n",
    "\n",
    "# merge all three\n",
    "score_df = granger_df[['emission', 'C']]\\\n",
    "    .merge(ccf_df[['emission', 'L_score']], on='emission')\\\n",
    "    .merge(irf_df[['emission', 'cum_response']], on='emission')\n",
    "\n",
    "# normalize\n",
    "score_df['C_norm'] = minmax(score_df['C'])\n",
    "score_df['L_norm'] = minmax(score_df['L_score'])\n",
    "score_df['E_norm'] = minmax(score_df['cum_response'])\n",
    "\n",
    "# equal weights — adjust if needed\n",
    "w1, w2, w3 = 1/3, 1/3, 1/3\n",
    "score_df['CIS'] = (\n",
    "    w1 * minmax(score_df['C']) +\n",
    "    w2 * minmax(score_df['L_score']) +\n",
    "    w3 * minmax(score_df['cum_response'])\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "score_df['CIS'] /= score_df['CIS'].sum()\n",
    "score_df = score_df.sort_values('CIS', ascending=False).reset_index(drop=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "# clean display\n",
    "score_df[['emission', 'C_norm', 'L_norm', 'E_norm', 'CIS']].round(3)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
