  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Time-series Models"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Time-series models are specifically designed to capture temporal dependencies and trends in sequential data. They can model the underlying structure of the data, such as seasonality, trends, and autocorrelation, which are common in climate data. By leveraging these temporal patterns, time-series models can provide more accurate predictions of future temperature changes based on historical emissions data."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "ds_global = pd.read_csv(ROOT + \"final_global_ds.csv\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "ds_global"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### LSTM "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "LSTM (Long Short-Term Memory) is a type of recurrent neural network (RNN) that is well-suited for modeling sequential data, such as time series. LSTMs are designed to capture long-term dependencies in the data, which can be particularly important when analyzing climate data that may have complex temporal patterns."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Preprocessing"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We tried many different features engineering techniques, including cumulative features, lag features, rolling features, and more. However, none of these techniques seem to improve the performance of the model, which suggests that they introduce noise rather than useful information for the model to learn from (like in Random Forest). \n",
    "\n",
    "Only features that we add is temperature lag features, which is the temperature of the previous year. This is because temperature is a variable that has strong temporal dependencies, and including lag features can help the model capture these dependencies more effectively. However, we want our model to be able to predict even without temperature lag features, since in real life, we may not have access to the previous year's temperature data when making predictions for the future."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "df = ds_country.copy()\n",
    "\n",
    "df = df.sort_values([\"country\", \"year\"])\n",
    "\n",
    "# for gas in gas_cols:\n",
    "#     df[f\"{gas}_cumulative\"] = df.groupby(\"country\")[gas].cumsum()\n",
    "\n",
    "# lags = [1, 2, 3, 5, 10]\n",
    "\n",
    "# for gas in gas_cols:\n",
    "#     for lag in lags:\n",
    "#         df[f\"{gas}_lag_{lag}\"] = df.groupby(\"country\")[gas].shift(lag)\n",
    "\n",
    "# window = 10\n",
    "\n",
    "# for gas in gas_cols:\n",
    "#     df[f\"{gas}_roll_mean_{window}\"] = (\n",
    "#         df.groupby(\"country\")[gas]\n",
    "#         .transform(lambda x: x.rolling(window, min_periods=1).mean())\n",
    "#     )\n",
    "\n",
    "# for gas in gas_cols:\n",
    "#     df[f\"{gas}_roll_sum_{window}\"] = (\n",
    "#         df.groupby(\"country\")[gas]\n",
    "#         .transform(lambda x: x.rolling(window, min_periods=1).sum())\n",
    "#     )\n",
    "\n",
    "# df[\"total_emissions\"] = df[gas_cols].sum(axis=1)\n",
    "# df[\"total_emissions_cum\"] = df.groupby(\"country\")[\"total_emissions\"].cumsum()\n",
    "\n",
    "# df[\"year_norm\"] = df[\"year\"] - df[\"year\"].min()\n",
    "\n",
    "# df[\"decade\"] = (df[\"year\"] // 10) * 10\n",
    "\n",
    "df[\"temp_lag_1\"] = df.groupby(\"country\")[\"AvgTemp\"].shift(1)\n",
    "\n",
    "df = df.dropna().reset_index(drop=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "unseen_countries = [\"Singapore\"]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We process the data to create sequences of input features and corresponding target values for training the LSTM model:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Fit a global StandardScaler on the training data to prevent data leakage and ensure that the model can generalize to new countries without needing country-specific scalers.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Loop through records from each country in the dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "- Scale the emissions data for each country using global StandardScaler to ensure that the features are on a similar scale, which can help improve the performance of the model.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Create sequences of x years of emissions data as input features, and the corresponding average temperature of the next year as the target variable.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Split the sequences into training and testing sets based on a time-based split, ensuring that the model is trained on historical data and tested on more recent data.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Collect years and country information for the test set to visualize the predictions later."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Before this approach, we tried using one scaler for each country, but we found that it was harder to train the model, and we can't use it to predict the temperature of a new country, since we don't have the scaler for that country. By using a global scaler with latitude and longitude information, we can ensure that the model can generalize better to new countries and also capture the overall trends in the data more effectively.\n",
    "\n",
    "We set window_size to 5, which means that the model will use the emissions data from the past 5 years to predict the average temperature of the next year. We have tried different window sizes (e.g., 10, 20, 25), and we found that 5 gives us the best performance. This suggests that the model favors short-term dependencies in the data, which may be due to the fact that temperature changes can be influenced by recent emissions more than older emissions or characteristics of the LSTM architecture."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "window_size = 5  # number of past years to use\n",
    "\n",
    "# Function to create sequences for time-series modeling\n",
    "def create_sequences(X, y, window_size):\n",
    "    Xs, ys = [], []\n",
    "    for i in range(len(X) - window_size):\n",
    "        Xs.append(X[i:i + window_size])\n",
    "        ys.append(y[i + window_size])\n",
    "    return np.array(Xs), np.array(ys)\n",
    "\n",
    "feature_cols = df.drop(columns=['country', 'ISO3', 'continent', 'AvgTemp']).columns.tolist()\n",
    "df_features_all = df[feature_cols]\n",
    "df_target_all = df['AvgTemp'].values\n",
    "\n",
    "# calculate cutoff year for training (80% of the timeline)\n",
    "cutoff_year = df['year'].unique()[int(0.8 * (len(df['year'].unique()) - window_size))] + window_size\n",
    "\n",
    "# Use a global scaler across all numeric features\n",
    "scaler_X = StandardScaler()\n",
    "scaler_y = StandardScaler()\n",
    "scaler_X.fit(df_features_all[df['year'] <= cutoff_year])\n",
    "scaler_y.fit(df_target_all.reshape(-1, 1)[df['year'] <= cutoff_year])\n",
    "\n",
    "X_list_train, X_list_test = [], []\n",
    "y_list_train, y_list_test = [], []\n",
    "country_test = []\n",
    "year_test_list = []\n",
    "\n",
    "temp_idx = feature_cols.index('temp_lag_1')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Loop through each country, scale using the global scaler, create sequences, and split\n",
    "for country in df['country'].unique():\n",
    "    df_country = df[df['country'] == country].sort_values('year')\n",
    "    df_features = df_country.drop(columns=['country','ISO3','continent','AvgTemp'])\n",
    "    df_target = df_country['AvgTemp'].values.reshape(-1, 1)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Scale features globally\n",
    "    X_scaled = scaler_X.transform(df_features)\n",
    "    y_scaled = scaler_y.transform(df_target).flatten()\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Create sequences\n",
    "    X_country, y_country = create_sequences(X_scaled, y_scaled, window_size)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    if country in unseen_countries:\n",
    "        split_seq = 0  # Use all data for testing\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        split_seq = int(len(X_country) * 0.8) # 80/20 split for train/test\n",
    "    \n",
    "    X_list_train.append(X_country[:split_seq])\n",
    "    y_list_train.append(y_country[:split_seq])\n",
    "    \n",
    "    X_list_test.append(X_country[split_seq:])\n",
    "    y_list_test.append(y_country[split_seq:])\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Track test countries and years\n",
    "    country_test.extend([country] * len(y_country[split_seq:]))\n",
    "    \n",
    "    years = df_country['year'].values\n",
    "    seq_years = years[window_size:]  # align with y\n",
    "    years_test = seq_years[split_seq:]\n",
    "    year_test_list.extend(years_test)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We convert all data into PyTorch tensors and create DataLoader objects for efficient batching during training."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Combine all countries' train and test data into single tensors for modeling\n",
    "X_train = torch.tensor(np.concatenate(X_list_train), dtype=torch.float32)\n",
    "y_train = torch.tensor(np.concatenate(y_list_train), dtype=torch.float32)\n",
    "\n",
    "X_test = torch.tensor(np.concatenate(X_list_test), dtype=torch.float32)\n",
    "y_test = torch.tensor(np.concatenate(y_list_test), dtype=torch.float32)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Create DataLoaders for batch processing during model training\n",
    "train_loader = DataLoader(TensorDataset(X_train, y_train), batch_size=128, shuffle=True)\n",
    "test_loader = DataLoader(TensorDataset(X_test, y_test), batch_size=128, shuffle=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "year_test_all = np.array(year_test_list)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"Total test samples:\", sum(len(x) for x in X_list_test))\n",
    "print(\"Test years:\", min(years_test), \"-\", max(years_test))\n",
    "print(\"Unique test countries:\", len(set(country_test)))\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(\"Total train samples:\", sum(len(x) for x in X_list_train))\n",
    "print(\"Unique train countries:\", len(set(df['country'].unique()) - set(unseen_countries)))\n",
    "print(\"Number of features:\", X_train.shape[2])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Model Creation and Training"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We build a simple LSTM model using PyTorch, which consists of an LSTM layer followed by a fully connected layer to output the predicted temperature."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Define the LSTM model\n",
    "class ClimateLSTM(nn.Module):\n",
    "    def __init__(\n",
    "        self,\n",
    "        input_size,\n",
    "        hidden_size=64,\n",
    "        num_layers=2,\n",
    "        dropout=0.2\n",
    "    ):\n",
    "        super().__init__()\n",
    "\n",
    "        self.lstm = nn.LSTM(\n",
    "            input_size=input_size,\n",
    "            hidden_size=hidden_size,\n",
    "            num_layers=num_layers,\n",
    "            batch_first=True,\n",
    "            dropout=dropout if num_layers > 1 else 0.0\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "        # Simple normalization (stability)\n",
    "        self.norm = nn.LayerNorm(hidden_size)\n",
    "\n",
    "        # Lightweight prediction head\n",
    "        self.head = nn.Sequential(\n",
    "            nn.Linear(hidden_size, 64),\n",
    "            nn.ReLU(),\n",
    "            nn.Dropout(dropout),\n",
    "            nn.Linear(64, 1)\n",
    "        )\n",
    "\n",
    "    def forward(self, x):\n",
    "        # x: (batch, seq_len, features)\n",
    "        out, _ = self.lstm(x)\n",
    "        \n",
    "        # Use LAST timestep only\n",
    "        last = out[:, -1, :]\n",
    "        last = self.norm(last)\n",
    "        return self.head(last)\n",
    "\n",
    "input_size = X_train.shape[2]\n",
    "model = ClimateLSTM(input_size)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Move model to GPU if available\n",
    "device = torch.device(\"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"xpu\" if torch.xpu.is_available() else \"cpu\")\n",
    "model.to(device)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We use:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Hubeer loss (L1 function) to measure the difference between the predicted and actual temperatures. The Huber loss is less sensitive to outliers than MSE, which can be beneficial when dealing with climate data that may have extreme values.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Adam optimizer to update the model weights during training.\n",
    "\n",
    "- ReduceLROnPlateau learning rate scheduler to reduce the learning rate if the validation loss does not improve for a certain number of epochs."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "criterion = nn.SmoothL1Loss()\n",
    "optimizer = torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=1e-3, weight_decay=1e-4)\n",
    "scheduler = torch.optim.lr_scheduler.ReduceLROnPlateau(optimizer, 'min', patience=10, factor=0.5)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We train model for a specified number of epochs, with early stopping based on validation loss to prevent overfitting. We alsp include 50% chance of changing temperature lag features to 0.0 (mean value after scaling), to make the model more robust and not rely too much on temperature lag features, since in real life, we may not have access to the previous year's temperature data when making predictions for the future.\n",
    "\n",
    "Training can be time-consuming, especially with a large dataset and complex model architecture. You can expect 5-10 minutes running this in a normal Google Colab environment."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "epochs = 200\n",
    "best_loss = float('inf')\n",
    "patience = 50\n",
    "counter = 0\n",
    "\n",
    "train_losses = []\n",
    "val_losses = []\n",
    "\n",
    "# Training loop \n",
    "for epoch in range(epochs):\n",
    "    model.train()\n",
    "    train_loss = 0\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Loop through batches of training data\n",
    "    for X_batch, y_batch in train_loader:\n",
    "        X_batch, y_batch = X_batch.to(device), y_batch.to(device)\n",
    "\n",
    "        if torch.rand(1).item() < 0.5:  # 50% chance to not include temp lag\n",
    "            X_batch[:, :, temp_idx] = 0.0  \n",
    "\n",
    "        # Calculate loss and backpropagate\n",
    "        optimizer.zero_grad()\n",
    "        output = model(X_batch).squeeze()\n",
    "        loss = criterion(output, y_batch)\n",
    "        loss.backward()\n",
    "        optimizer.step()\n",
    "        train_loss += loss.item()\n",
    "    \n",
    "    train_loss /= len(train_loader)\n",
    "    train_losses.append(train_loss)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Validation\n",
    "    model.eval()\n",
    "    val_loss = 0\n",
    "    with torch.no_grad():\n",
    "        # Loop through batches of test data to calculate validation loss\n",
    "        for X_batch, y_batch in test_loader:\n",
    "            X_batch, y_batch = X_batch.to(device), y_batch.to(device)\n",
    "\n",
    "            if torch.rand(1).item() < 0.5:  # 50% chance to not include temp lag\n",
    "                X_batch[:, :, temp_idx] = 0.0  \n",
    "\n",
    "            output = model(X_batch).squeeze()\n",
    "            loss = criterion(output, y_batch)\n",
    "            val_loss += loss.item()\n",
    "    \n",
    "    val_loss /= len(test_loader)\n",
    "    val_losses.append(val_loss)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Early stopping logic\n",
    "    if val_loss < best_loss:\n",
    "        # If validation loss improves, save the model and reset the counter\n",
    "        best_loss = val_loss\n",
    "        counter = 0\n",
    "        best_model = model.state_dict()\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        counter += 1\n",
    "    \n",
    "    if counter >= patience:\n",
    "        print(f\"Early stopping at epoch {epoch+1}\")\n",
    "        break\n",
    "    \n",
    "    if (epoch + 1) % 10 == 0:\n",
    "        print(f\"Epoch {epoch+1}, Train Loss: {train_loss:.5f}, Val Loss: {val_loss:.5f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "model.load_state_dict(best_model)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "plt.plot(train_losses, label=\"Train Loss\")\n",
    "plt.plot(val_losses, label=\"Validation Loss\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Epoch\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Loss\")\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.title(\"Training vs Validation Loss\")\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can see that validation loss are very volatile, which suggests that the model is struggling to learn from the data. This could be due to several factors, such as the complexity of temperature dynamics, the presence of noise in the data, or the model architecture not being well-suited for this task. "
